Someone asks, “What should we do on Saturday?” One friend suggests dinner, another sends a cinema link, and a third replies to a message from yesterday. There are plenty of ideas, but still no plan.

A group poll gives that conversation somewhere to land. Ask one question, offer a short list of choices, and put everyone's vote in the same place. Here is how to make a poll that helps your group decide.

## Start with one decision

“What are we doing this weekend?” leaves a lot open. “Where should we have dinner on Saturday?” gives people something specific to answer.

Include the details that affect the choice: the day, a rough time, a location, or a budget. If you need to choose both a date and an activity, use separate polls. Otherwise, someone might vote for an activity they like on a day they cannot attend.

## Offer choices people can compare

A useful starting point is three or four realistic options. Give each one enough context to make the tradeoff clear:

- Pizza near the station, around €15 each.
- Taverna in the square, around €20 each.
- Dinner at home, with everyone bringing a dish.

Those prices are examples, not restaurant quotes. Replace them with details that fit your actual plan. Before opening the vote, check any constraints that would rule out an option, such as dietary needs or travel distance.

## Create and share the poll in Pickle

Create your poll in the Pickle Android app, add your question and choices, and share its link in your group chat. Friends can open the link and vote in their browser without installing the app.

Web voters choose a display name and an avatar before their first vote. They can participate as guests without registering an email account.

If you want more ideas, enable suggestions. You can choose whether suggested options need your approval before joining the vote. If the shortlist is already agreed, keep suggestions off so the decision stays focused.

## Set expectations before the votes arrive

Add a short message alongside the link:

> Vote for the dinner you'd prefer by Thursday evening. We'll check the result, confirm the booking, and post the final details here.

That deadline is an agreement with your group. Remember to close the poll yourself when voting is finished.

Pickle also lets the creator choose whether voters can change their vote and whether results are visible before voting. Allowing vote changes can help when plans shift. Hiding results until someone votes lets them make their initial choice without seeing the current leader.

## Finish with a clear confirmation

A leading option is a preference, not a reservation. Check availability and confirm any practical constraints before calling the plan settled. If the result is tied, agree on a deciding rule or run a second poll with the tied options.

Once the decision is made, close the poll and send one final message with the place, time, and anything people need to bring.

Need a question to start with? Try these [group dinner poll examples](/blog/group-dinner-poll-questions), or use separate votes to [plan a trip with friends](/blog/plan-a-trip-with-friends).

[See how Pickle works](/#how), then create your next group poll in the Android app and share the link with your crew.
